ive or who we want not to give anything  to and he talks about the development of  positive feelings towards those who  benefit us one of the most common of  these positive feelings is what we call  gratitude right so he quotes you know a  few people here  Polybius right who says when a man who  has been helped when endangered by  another design a tional gratitude to his  preserver but goes to the length of  attempting to do him injury it's clear  those who become aware of it will  naturally be displeased and offended by  such conduct sharing the resentment of  their injured neighbor and goes on and  on and on so he says that to say the  duty to repay benefits as the beginning  and end of justice is an overstatement  but that it is the beginning is  plausible right and wrong the notion of  what you ought to do those who actually  benefit you lose think about parents you  know giving you life nourishing you  raising you you should be grateful to  them you should be benevolent towards  them  there should be reciprocity your you  know brothers sister who hides your  stuff and picks on you and makes fun of  you with their friends maybe you know  kin altruism would say you still have to  be good to them because they're your  your kin but reciprocal altruism could  say screw you I'm not going to give you  anything you never benefit me you  actually treat me in bad ways so we have  positive feelings for those who would  benefit us and then we have negative  feelings towards those who don't  reciprocate and these negative feelings  can take the form of indignation and  they can even go to revenge and revenge  can be for not doing something for  example people get really angry when you  don't learn their names and the  is a big problem for me because I heart  remembering names including those even  of my students the older I get the the  more trouble I have with that and  monadic devices don't seem to help so I  just have to live with it right but I  should know that people 